h me you become  convinced that there can be objective  moral truths  this was in some ways opened up the  field to other possible ideas as to what  was intrinsically good  because now you could argue that  something was intrinsically good even  was not  something that people preferred and in  that light i went back to reading  some of the classical utilitarians again  particularly siddwick and his arguments  for why happiness rather than the  satisfaction of desires  is the ultimate value something that is  of intrinsic value  and it did seem to overcome these  problems with preference utilitarianism  that had been troubling me  it certainly had some paradoxes of its  own  some things that it seemed not to handle  as well but  you know after thinking about it again i  decided that it was  more likely than not that a hedonistic  few was the right view  i wouldn't put it stronger than that i  still think preference utilitarianism  has some things to be said for it and  there are also of course  views that say yes happiness is  intrinsically good and suffering is  intrinsically bad but they're not the  only things that are intrinsically good  or bad  things like justice or freedom or  whatever  you know there's various other  candidates that people have put forward  many of them in fact for  being objectively good or bad so there  are also possibilities  when you mention that happiness or  certain sorts of conscious states of  sentient creatures can be seen as  intrinsically good or valuable keeping  in mind sort of  the moral realism that you hold what is  the  metaphysical status of experiences  in the universe given this view is it  that happiness is good based off of  the application of reason in the  rational space of ethics  unpack the ontology of happiness and the  metaphysics here a bit  but of course it doesn't change what  happiness is that's to say that it's of  intrinsic value but that is the  claim that i'm making that the world is  a better place  if it 